\description{
Fixed-effect (population-level) predictions: the linear predictor is
\verb{X beta} with random effects held at their population mean (zero).
On the identity link the response- and link-scale predictions
coincide. This is the prediction surface \pkg{marginaleffects} uses
for average predictions and slopes.
}
\details{
With \code{gen} random, \code{classify = "gen"} returns each genotype's fixed part
plus its random effect, and on a multi-environment fit with \code{gen:env}
random it also adds that genotype's \code{gen:env} effects averaged over
environments. This matches ASReml's default \code{predict()}: equal-weight
averaging over the fixed-term variables not in \code{classify}, a covariate
held at its mean, variables that appear only in random terms ignored, and
every random term whose variables are all in \code{classify} or averaged over
included. The intervals integrate over INLA's hyperparameter
configurations, so they include the uncertainty in the variance
components. A table with no random term is computed by emmeans, with
intervals from the Gaussian approximation of the fixed-effect
posterior. The arguments of
ASReml's \code{predict()} that change these rules (\code{present}, \code{average},
\code{ignore}, \code{use}, \code{except}, \code{only}, \code{associate}, \code{levels}, \code{vcov}, \code{sed},
\code{parallel}, \code{aliased}, \code{design.points}) are refused with
\code{classify_argument_not_supported}.

With \code{allow_new_levels = TRUE}, the prediction for a set of rows is the
mean response over them, observation noise included, as an observed trial
or cell mean at a new locality would be. Given one posterior draw it is
normal, with mean \verb{x'beta} averaged over the rows plus the mean of the
effects at levels the fit saw, and variance the sum over random terms of
\code{sigma^2 * sum_l (n_l / n)^2} over the unseen levels \code{l} (\code{n_l} of the \code{n}
rows at level \code{l}), plus \code{sigma_field^2} times the mean AR1 x AR1
correlation over pairs of rows in a trial the fit did not see, plus the
residual variance over \code{n}. The prediction is the mixture of these normals
over the draws: \code{estimate} and \code{sd} are its exact mean and standard
deviation, and \code{lower} and \code{upper} its quantiles. The fixed effects and the
seen effects are drawn jointly from INLA's approximation, and the variances
from their continuous joint posterior (as \code{\link[=fb_variance_draws]{fb_variance_draws()}} draws
them). Every random number comes from R's generator, so \code{set.seed()} before
the call makes the result repeatable.

The new-level draw is built for random intercepts, interactions
(\code{TRIAL:REP}), \code{diag(f):g} and \code{id(trial):ar1(row):ar1(col)}, over an
independent residual or \code{dsum(~ units | f)}, for a Gaussian response on the
identity link. Any other structure is refused with
\code{predict_new_levels_structure_not_supported}, a new level with no fitted
variance (a new level of \code{f} in \code{diag(f):g}, a new residual section, a row
or column off the fitted lattice) with \code{predict_new_levels_no_variance},
and another family with \code{predict_new_levels_family_not_supported}.

On a fit with \code{fixed_alias = "drop"}, a row that is not a linear
combination of the rows the model was fitted to has no estimable
prediction: it is returned as \code{NA}, and the \code{status} attribute (a
\code{status} element with \code{se.fit = TRUE}, a \code{status} column with
\code{classify}) reads \code{"Aliased"} there and \code{"Estimable"} elsewhere, as in
asreml's \code{predict()}.
}
